    <h2 class="float_clear">🛠️ Common tools</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Queue management systems</li>
        <li>Human resources systems</li>
        <li>Public lists of known issues</li>
        <li>Internal procedures</li>
        <li>DevOps, Agile, and other methodologies</li>
        <li>Open source repositories</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>📊 Metrics</h2>
    {{ include('supops/metrics/sustainability.twig') }}
    <h2>💡 Tips</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Build everything with security and privacy in mind.</li>
        <li>Don't release on Fridays or before public holidays unless there is a <em>very good</em> reason for it.
        </li>
        <li>Monitor the program when you release something to production so that you do not wake up in a panic.</li>
        <li>Have Continuity of Business (COB) procedures covering hardware, software, services', people, and potentially other types of failures (keep them as standardized as possible).</li>
        <li>If the service is down, either switch to something else, where possible, or provide clear communication that is down, why it is down, and for how long (if that is known).</li>
        <li>Limit the number of active tickets per agent (can vary, but 10 is probably the most that the majority of people can handle more or less efficiently).</li>
        <li>Have enough people to avoid understaffing critical functions for a prolonged time even when 2 people are missing (1 on vacation and 1 on sick leave).</li>
        <li>Encourage taking vacation days instead of hoarding them.</li>
        <li>Be careful with alerts so that they do not become noise that is being ignored.</li>
        <li>Encourage smaller iterative changes instead of big ones.</li>
        <li>Once an issue is fixed, explore ways to prevent recurrence in mid- and long-terms (create items in the backlog, if required).</li>
        <li>Try to consider scaling of the solutions in time or at least with the ability to handle sudden influxes (during season events, for example).</li>
        <li>Promote learning of new things inside the company, both about the product and related areas of knowledge.</li>
        <li>If you realize that you are no longer able to support the product (or a feature), find ways to hand it over to the community, in case someone else would be willing to. For example, open source the code, release SDKs or binaries.</li>
        <li>Have regularly updated onboarding training for new hires (bonus points if they are
            <a href="/talks/threads/1884" target="_blank">gamified</a>).
        </li>
        <li>Security and privacy-related issues should always take the highest priority.</li>
        <li>Keep calm and carry-on: cool head is what solves the problems that hot heads have created, so punch some boxing dummy and approach everything logically.</li>
        <li>Do try to use eco-friendly solutions and generally be mindful about the environment.</li>
    </ul>
